    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d36">

                <pb facs="0130" n="884" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp02v02/pg884"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/3750</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to Mr.
                            Ira Jewell Williams, Philadelphia</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-06-22">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 22, 1917</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department acknowledges the receipt of
                    your letters of May 28 and June 6, 1917,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d36-f1"/> in
                    which, on behalf of your clients, The Atlantic Refining Co., you seek advice as
                    to whether they would be justified in shipping petroleum to certain firms in St.
                    Vincent, Cape Verde.</p>
                <p>In reply you are advised that this Department is in receipt of information to the
                    effect that Joaquin de St. Maurice, of St. Vincent, Cape Verde, is, in
                    consequence of his intercourse with Hamburg and his relations with the North
                    German Lloyd, said to be
                    under grave suspicion.</p>
                <p>It has been made known to the Department that the firm, known as Antonio Miguel
                    de Carvalho &amp; Co., which, apparently, has recently taken over the business
                    of Joaquin de St. Maurice, is under like suspicion, and that there is danger
                    that the consignment of petroleum, to which you refer, if shipped to this
                    company, would be used to the advantage of the enemy.</p>
                <p>As bearing on the matter, the Department may call your attention to the rule
                    enunciated by American courts to the effect that intercourse, either directly or
                    indirectly, between residents of enemy countries is illegal; also to the fact
                    that there is now pending before Congress, as you probably are aware, a bill (H.
                    R. 4704) with regard to trade with the enemy. A copy of the bill is enclosed
                        herewith.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d36-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Frank L. Polk</hi>
                        
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Counselor</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
